    "turn_idx": 91,
    "turn_text": "No, I thought... Yeah, Ms. Dickerson, she was said, but can we middle school? And the first day of school, we would state our name and what we wanted to be in the future. And everybody would stand up and state their name. And I wanted to be a police officer. And when my turn, I was, fields was my last name, and they were doing it in alphabetical order. So when they got to me, the few kids in front of me said something big. They said doctors, and I just wanted to be the biggest thing. So when they, as Dickers said, she pointed at me and called my name, and I said, my name is Cleo Fields, and the only thing that came into my mind was president. And I said, I want to be president when I grow up. And everybody in the class will have. You know, including Ms. Dickinson. And it was a funny moment, but for the class, but for me, it was a purpose-driven moment. And I just wanted to know, how could I become president? That's how it all started. That's how my whole public service life started. I had no, nobody in my family had any interest in politics. Nobody would, you know, no politicians, none of that. And I just started focusing, and I started running for office as a student, you know, and I became vice president of the class, and then I became president of my freshman class at Southern, and then a student body president, and then I decided, look, you know, I got to go run further. go to Senate because to be president, I was always, you know, everything I had read, presidents were lawyers. the modern day present. And they were former congressmen, former governors, former senators. And you had a better chance if you were a former governor or a former US senator. And so that was my path. And that's how my whole public service started. It all started with a laugh. Fast forward",
